description:
2.0.0 removes every API whose deprecation message says 'Removed in Kinieta 2.0':
- the vendored HandyUIKit colour helpers (UIColorExtension.swift): ChangeableColorComponent, UIColor(hue:luminance:chroma:alpha:), hlca, hsba, rgba, change(_:by:), change(_:to:);
- UIColor.Components (UIColor+Components.swift);
- the Block typealias, and the @_disfavoredOverload onComplete and group overloads that take a plain () -> Void (Namespace.swift);
- the then property;
- Easing.Curve.custom;
- Bezier.solve(_:) and Bezier.Point(_:_:).

Update the tests that used them, the DocC article DeprecatedColorHelpers and its topic link, the README, and add CHANGELOG 'Removed' entries naming each replacement.

Users build a timeline with a modal grammar, and much of the code exists to support that grammar.

## What is wrong for the user

- Postfix calls edit the step before them: easing, easeIn/Out/InOut, delay and onComplete reach back to the previous step. What they affect depends on what came before and on whether that step has started yet. delay, easing, onComplete and parallel() are each documented as doing nothing once their step has started, and debug builds need IgnoredCall warnings to tell users when a call hit nothing.
- parallel() gathers every step since the last then() or parallel(), and then() exists only to put a fence in for parallel(). Internally it marks the fence with a group holding one sequence (Kinieta.seal, ActionQueue.popAllUngrouped).
- A timeline is not a value. A pulse cannot be defined once and run on several views. Running several views together needs Kinieta.group over live handles, with membership rules users have to learn: at most one group, finished handles left out, members leave when a paused group's handle is released, finished members rejoin their group.
- The Kinieta handle is the builder, the playback controller and the group container all at once.

## The pattern

Build timelines as a composite of step values: animate, wait, call, sequence and parallel. A result builder composes them, and a modifier returns a new value instead of editing a queue, like SwiftUI's view tree or a CAAnimationGroup. The handle only plays and controls. Most of the tree already exists: ActionType is a composite of descriptions that become live actions when they start. The builder is a public face on that tree, so nothing that runs per frame changes.

kinieta-84p.7 (Step, StepBuilder, view.run) and kinieta-84p.5 (Kinieta.run, view.step) specify the API; it looks like this:

    let pulse = Step.sequence {
        Step.animate(.alpha(0.3), duration: 0.8, easing: .inOut(.sine))
        Step.animate(.alpha(1), duration: 0.8, easing: .inOut(.sine))
    }
    badge.run(pulse.repeatForever())

    view.run {
        Step.animate(.x(300), duration: 1)
        Step.parallel {
            Step.animate(.x(200), duration: 1, easing: .inOut(.cubic))
            Step.animate(.alpha(0), duration: 0.2, delay: 0.8)
        }
        Step.call { print("done") }
    }

    Kinieta.run {                                  // several views, one handle
        Step.parallel {
            card.step(.x(374), duration: 1, easing: .inOut(.cubic))
            badge.step(.rotation(degrees: 360), .alpha(0), duration: 1.2)
        }
    }

The chain API stays as it is through 1.x. Whether parts of it are deprecated for 2.0 is decided in kinieta-84p.8.

## Performance stance (applies to every child)

Composing happens at build time: the result builder resolves at compile time, and the output is the same ActionType tree as today. Nothing changes in the per-frame paths: PropertyAnimation.update, Bezier.progress(at:), the colour interpolators, and Engine.update(with:). Refactors are gated by the per-frame benchmark ticket: record its numbers before and after in the close reason.

Record that public API follows the Swift API Design Guidelines (https://www.swift.org/documentation/api-design-guidelines/), so new tickets are designed against them and not reviewed after the fact (the api-guidelines tickets came from such a review).

Add a bullet to 'Conventions & Patterns' in CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md (keep both in sync), and to .orchestra/worker-prompt.md if it lists conventions. Keep it short and list only what Kinieta enforces:
- every public declaration has a doc comment that opens with a summary;
- methods without side effects read as noun phrases, and methods with side effects as verbs (chain calls such as then() and parallel() are the established DSL exception);
- first arguments that form a prepositional phrase are labelled at the preposition, and weakly typed arguments, such as a bare Double, are labelled by role;
- shipped API is renamed through @available(*, deprecated, renamed:) and removed in 2.0, while API not yet in a tag is renamed freely.

Points the review decided against, so they are not raised again: renaming then()/parallel()/easing(_:) to imperative verbs (churn on the 1.0 chain DSL); a make prefix for Kinieta.group or Easing.in/out (static-member precedent); labels on Bezier's four control points (CSS precedent); .background versus .backgroundColor (SwiftUI precedent).
